{"id":"8c82c91d-5303-475b-9c2e-111e18abab7d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785014873,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\\n    selected = []\\n    intervals = []\\n    for c in sorted_calls:\\n        s = c['start']\\n        e = c['end']\\n        overlap = False\\n        for (sel_s, sel_e) in intervals:\\n            if not (e <= sel_s or s >= sel_e):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(c)\\n            intervals.append((s, e))\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True):\\n        s = c['start']\\n        e = c['end']\\n        rep = c['replacement_tokens']\\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:s] + rep + result_tokens[e:]\\n    return {'selected_call_ids': selected_ids, 'tokens': result_tokens}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for AGENT-TF-02 based on the description. The task: \"Apply accepted API calls to text while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, and keeping all eligible calls.\" The input payload has tokens (list of strings) and calls (list of objects with call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens). We need to select a subset of calls (non-overlapping) and then produce the interleaved token stream after replacements. The selection criteria: rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID. Greedily reject any span overlapping an already selected span. Order accepted calls by source start for reporting. Apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift. Return selected_call_ids (list of call_ids in order of start) and tokens (the resulting token list after applying replacements). The replacement_tokens are inserted at the start position, replacing the span from start to end (exclusive? Usually start inclusive, end exclusive? But in examples: tokens indices 0-9, call a: start=1, end=3, replacement_tokens: [\"<a>\"]. That would replace tokens[1] and tokens[2] with one token? Yes, the output should be a sequence of tokens. The replacement tokens replace the span. So we need to apply them in order from rightmost to leftmost to avoid offset shifts. The result tokens should be the interleaved token stream. Also note that the output schema requires selected_call_ids and tokens. Both arrays of strings.\n\nWe need to write Python code that follows these rules. Let's outline:\n\n1. Input case: a dictionary with keys 'tokens' (list of strings) and 'calls' (list of dicts). Validate required fields (maybe not necessary but good practice). We'll assume they are present.\n\n2. Sort calls by descending score, then ascending start (since start position, if ties, ascending? The instruction says \"descending score, start position, then call ID\". Typically for ties, compare start then call_id. But \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Means primary key: score descending; secondary: start position; tertiary: call ID. So sort key: (-score, start, call_id). Use appropriate types.\n\n3. Greedily select calls: iterate over sorted calls, maintain a set of occupied indices? Since we need to check overlap, we can maintain a list of intervals (start, end) for selected calls. For each call, check if its interval [start, end) overlaps with any selected interval. If no overlap, select it. Overlap condition: intervals overlap if not (call.end <= selected.start or call.start >= selected.end). Since start inclusive, end exclusive? The fixture: start=1, end=3 means indices 1 and 2 (2 tokens). So it's half-open [start, end). We'll assume that. So we need to be consistent.\n\n4. After selecting, sort selected calls by start position (ascending). Then apply replacements from right to left (i.e., descending start). For each selected call, replace the slice tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens. The replacement tokens list may contain multiple tokens. We need to modify a list of tokens in place. Since we apply rightmost first, we avoid index shifts. So sort selected calls by start descending. For each, do: tokens = tokens[:start] + replacement_tokens + tokens[end:].\n\n5. Return dict with selected_call_ids (ordered by start ascending) and tokens (the final list of strings). Note that tokens should be strings as per schema.\n\nEdge cases: what if there are no selected calls? Then selected_call_ids empty list, tokens unchanged. Also ensure that tokens are strings; replacement_tokens are list of strings. The output tokens must be list of strings.\n\nImplementation must be pure Python standard library. No external libs.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON-escaped string. The entire response must be a single JSON object.\n\nLet's write the code. We'll define solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case['tokens']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # Validate fields: each call must have call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. We'll assume.\n    # Sort calls:\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    selected = []\n    # intervals list for selected\n    intervals = []  # list of (start, end)\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        s = c['start']\n        e = c['end']\n        # check overlap with any selected\n        overlap = False\n        for (sel_s, sel_e) in intervals:\n            if not (e <= sel_s or s >= sel_e):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(c)\n            intervals.append((s, e))\n    # Now order selected by start ascending\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\n    # Apply replacements from rightmost to leftmost\n    # Create a copy of tokens to modify\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    # sort selected by start descending\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True):\n        s = c['start']\n        e = c['end']\n        rep = c['replacement_tokens']\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:s] + rep + result_tokens[e:]\n    return {'selected_call_ids': selected_ids, 'tokens': result_tokens}\n\nThat's it. Note: We need to ensure tokens are strings; replacement_tokens are list of strings from input.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON with the implementation string. We'll escape backslashes, newlines, quotes. In Python, we can use json.dumps to create the string representation of the code, but careful: the code itself contains quotes. Better to write the code as a multi-line string and then use json.dumps to escape. But we are constructing the final JSON object manually.\n\nWe'll write the implementation string as a Python string with double quotes, and escape necessary characters. For simplicity, we can use triple quotes in the source but inside JSON string we need to represent it.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\\n    selected = []\\n    intervals = []\\n    for c in sorted_calls:\\n        s = c['start']\\n        e = c['end']\\n        overlap = False\\n        for (sel_s, sel_e) in intervals:\\n            if not (e <= sel_s or s >= sel_e):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(c)\\n            intervals.append((s, e))\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]